🐛 Describe the bug
class Merge(torch.autograd.Function):
    @staticmethod
    def forward(ctx, x_0, x_1, fn_0, fn_1):
        ctx.x_0, ctx.x_1 = x_0, x_1
        ctx.y_0, ctx.y_1 = fn_0(x_0), fn_1(x_1)
        return ctx.y_0, ctx.y_1

    @staticmethod
    def backward(ctx: Any, dy_0, dy_1):
        dx_0 = torch.autograd.grad(ctx.y_0, ctx.x_0, grad_outputs=dy_0) # Autograd falls into Infinitive Recusive and ends with stack overflow
        dx_1 = torch.autograd.grad(ctx.y_1, ctx.x_1, grad_outputs=dy_1) # Autograd falls into Infinitive Recusive and ends with stack overflow
        return (dx_0, dx_1)
I want to define a custom op that merges two natural operations (fn_0 and fn_1) to compute together in not only forward pass but also backward pass.
The above code is what I want to achieve. But seems like torch.autograd.grad is not working in the backward function, because it recursively called the backward function again, thus results in python stack overflow.
What I expect is that torch.autograd.grad can help me to compute the correct grad(fn_0) and grad(fn_1).
Is there any work around to get over this bug?
Versions
torch >= 1.10.0_cuda10.0
